Q: Is it okay to have a varied structure definition and implementation? A: <p>What you need to do is <em>declare</em> the struct (i.e. state that it exists) in a header file that clients will use, and <em>define</em> it (i.e. specify the members) in the implementation file.</p>
<p>In order to do this, you do have to give the struct a tag so that the typedef matches the type in each file.</p>
<p>What you've shown is two different definitions, one with 4 members and one with 0 members, so those structs are incompatible.</p>
<p>So your public header file would have the following:</p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>typedef struct _string2 string2;
</code></pre>
<p>And your implementation file (which would also include the public header) would have this:</p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>struct _string2
{
    int length;

    int bufferLength;
    char* buffer;
    bool allocated;
};
</code></pre>

Q: Why would one use #include_next in a project? A: <p><code>include_next&lt;file.h&gt;</code> is used as a pre-processor directive to tell the compiler to exclude the search paths up to and including filename <code>file.h</code> from resolving to this header file. The typical need is when two header files of the same name need to be used. Use such features sparingly and only when absolutely necessary.</p>
<h3>For example:</h3>
<h4>Source <code>file.c</code> contents with the usual <code>file.h</code> from path 1:</h4>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>#include &lt;file.h&gt;

int main()
{
     printf(&quot;out value: %d&quot;, out_val);
     exit 0;
}
</code></pre>
<h4>file.h header file in path 1 contents with <code>file.h</code> from path 2 included:</h4>
<p><code>include_next</code> instructs that path 1 sub directory not be used as search path for <code>file.h</code> and instead use path 2 sub directory as search path. This way you can have 2 files of the same name without the fear of invoking a circular reference to itself.</p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>#include_next &lt;file.h&gt;

int out_val = UINT_MAX - INT_MAX;
</code></pre>
<h4>file.h in path 2 contents:</h4>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>#define INT_MAX 1&lt;&lt;63 - 1
#define UINT_MAX 1&lt;&lt;64 - 1
</code></pre>
